    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. You mentioned roundabouts \"absorb\" friction better. Can you expand on that? I'm trying to understand what specific types of 'friction' or systemic disruptions tend to cause the most significant drops in efficiency in these roundabout designs compared to signals.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Roundabouts are generally robust against minor disruptions, absorbing them by allowing continuous merging and weaving around the bottleneck; this smooth movement mitigates minor queue build-up. The most significant efficiency losses in roundabouts typically occur from severe geometric constraints, such as inadequate entry/exit sight lines or excessive splitter island length, which create forced stops. Unlike signals, which fail abruptly when timing is breached, a roundabout's failure mode is a gradual breakdown in flow caused by congestion reaching saturation points.",
      "source": "target",
